Every thread computes its own global index from its block and thread IDs, then uses that index to pick which array element it owns:

```c
__global__ void vecAdd(float *a, float *b, float *c, int n) {
    int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (i < n) {
        c[i] = a[i] + b[i];
    }
}
// Launch: vecAdd<<<numBlocks, threadsPerBlock>>>(a, b, c, n);
```

No loop over `n` appears anywhere — `n` threads (rounded up to whole blocks) run this same body once each, and `blockIdx`/`threadIdx` are what turn "one thread's logic" into "the whole array processed." The `if (i < n)` guard exists because the last block is often only partially full.
